170 :                         base64.setInitialBuffer(b, offset, len);
171 :                     }
172 :                     if (doEncode) {
173 :                         base64.encode(buf, 0, c);
174 :                     } else {
175 :                         base64.decode(buf, 0, c);
176 :                     }
177 :                 }
178 :             return base64.readResults(b, offset, len);
179 :         }
180 :     }"

"73 :     public Base64InputStream(InputStream in, boolean doEncode) {
74 :         super(in);
75 :         this.doEncode = doEncode;
76 :         this.base64 = new Base64(false);
77 :     }"

The `Base64InputStream` class has a `read(byte[], int, int)` method that reads bytes into the buffer array, starting at the specified offset and reading length number of bytes. In the implementation of this method, there is a condition checking if `len == 0`, in which case, it returns 0 immediately.

Here is the relevant snippet from the `read(byte[], int, int)` method of the `Base64InputStream` class:

```java
146: if (len == 0) {
147:     return 0;
148: }
```

This condition returns 0 when the specified length is 0, which explains why the method can return 0 in certain cases.

Additionally, the `Base64InputStream` class has a constructor that takes an `InputStream` and a boolean flag for encoding. Here is the constructor snippet:

```java
73: public Base64InputStream(InputStream in, boolean doEncode) {
74:     super(in);
75:     this.doEncode = doEncode;
76:     this.base64 = new Base64(false);
77: }
```

The constructor initializes the `Base64InputStream` with the given `InputStream` and sets the encoding flag accordingly.

The bug report describes that the issue occurs when using `new InputStreamReader(new Base64InputStream(in, true))`, which triggers the bug in the `Base64InputStream#read(byte[])` method. The method incorrectly returns 0 at the end of any stream which is a multiple of 3 bytes long, leading to the observed "java.io.IOException: Underlying input stream returned zero bytes" exception.

The bug seems to be in the handling of zero-length reads in the `Base64InputStream#read(byte[], int, int)` method, causing the premature termination of the read operation.

